  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date federal contract obligations for NAICS 541990 total $7.4B. AwardCount for the period is 6,070 and averageAwardSize is $1.2M. Obligations fell 38.0% year over year. The Department of Defense leads buying with $2.5B, followed by the Department of Energy with $1.9B and the General Services Administration with $1.1B. BATTELLE ENERGY ALLIANCE, LLC is the largest vendor with $1.8B in obligations, followed by HII MISSION TECHNOLOGIES CORP at $512M and BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC at $447M. Fiscal year trends show a peak at $14.5B in FY23, then $14.2B in FY24, $11.9B in FY25, and $7.4B in FY26 to date. All figures are contract obligations, and current-year totals are partial. This snapshot shows concentration by a few agencies and vendors, a steep year-over-year decline in obligations, and a still-significant award count and average award size for the fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is buying across agencies?",
        "body": "Buying is concentrated. The Department of Defense accounts for $2.5B and the Department of Energy accounts for $1.9B, together representing a large share of the $7.4B total. The General Services Administration adds $1.1B, and the next seven agencies range from $745M down to $55.9M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are obligations among vendors?",
        "body": "Vendor obligations are heavily top-loaded. BATTELLE ENERGY ALLIANCE, LLC has $1.8B in obligations, more than any single agency except the Department of Defense. The next largest vendors are HII MISSION TECHNOLOGIES CORP at $512M and BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC at $447M, with eight other vendors ranging from $300M to $101M."
      }
    ]
